{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab33134c386f9930","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Platform: RHOAI 3.6-ea.1\n2\t\n3\t## Metadata\n4\t\n5\t- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n6\t- **Version**: 3.6-ea.1\n7\t- **Base Platform**: OpenShift Container Platform v4.x\n8\t- **Supported OCP Versions**: v4.16+\n9\t- **Supported CPU Architectures**: amd64\n10\t- **Total Shipped Container Images**: 72+\n11\t- **Components Analyzed**: 72\n12\t- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n13\t\n14\t## Platform Overview\n15\t\n16\tRed Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI) 3.6-ea.1 is a comprehensive AI/ML platform deployed on OpenShift Container Platform through the Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). The platform is orchestrated by rhods-operator, which manages the lifecycle of all platform components through a hierarchy of custom resources — DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs. Each major capability (model serving, pipelines, distributed workloads, notebooks, model registry, training, observability, guardrails, and models-as-a-service) is implemented by a dedicated module operator or controller that watches its component CR and reconciles downstream Kubernetes resources using kustomize-based manifest rendering through the shared odh-platform-utilities framework.\n17\t\n18\tThe platform ingress architecture is in active transition. The rhods-operator Gateway service controller provisions a central `data-science-gateway` using Gateway API (GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoutes) while maintaining backward compatibility through OpenShift Routes. kube-auth-proxy provides OIDC-authenticated access to the dashboard, and the Gateway controller conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically creating EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected. Models-as-a-Service introduces a parallel ingress path through Kuadrant AuthPolicy enforcement with API key, TokenReview, and optional OIDC JWT authentication at the Gateway level. Model-registry-operator implements dual ingress (Gateway API HTTPRoutes and OpenShift Routes) with kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication on backend pods.\n19\t\n20\tSecurity follows a defense-in-depth posture: all shipped Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptography through system OpenSSL, operators read the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to align their TLS configuration with platform-wide policy, admission webhooks validate and default CRD resources with fail-closed policies, and per-component RBAC is scoped through dedicated ServiceAccounts and ClusterRoles. Authentication is layered across gateway-level (kube-auth-proxy OIDC, Kuadrant AuthPolicy), pod-level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar), and application-level (Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview) enforcement points.\n21\t\n22\t## Component Relationships\n23\t\n24\t### Dependency Graph\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\trhods-operator\n28\t|-- kserve (runtime-integration)\n29\t|   `-- kube-rbac-proxy (unknown)\n30\t|-- model-registry (unknown)\n31\t|   `-- kserve (runtime-integration) (*)\n32\t`-- models-as-a-service (runtime-library)\n33\t    `-- kserve (runtime-integration) (*)\n34\t\n35\tLeaf components (no internal deps to/from other components):\n36\t  MLServer\n37\t  NeMo-Guardrails\n38\t  argo-workflows\n39\t  eval-hub\n40\t  fms-guardrails-orchestrator\n41\t  guardrails-regex-detector\n42\t  kube-auth-proxy\n43\t  llm-d-planner\n44\t  lm-evaluation-harness\n45\t  mcp-lifecycle-module-operator\n46\t  mcp-lifecycle-operator\n47\t  ml-metadata\n48\t  model-metadata-collection\n49\t  odh-cli\n50\t  odh-observability\n51\t  ogx-k8s-operator\n52\t  OpenShell\n53\t  rhaii-cluster-validation\n54\t  spark-operator\n55\t  trainer\n56\t  trainer-operator\n57\t  training_hub\n58\t  training-operator\n59\t  vllm-cpu\n60\t  workload-variant-autoscaler\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\t### Central Components\n64\t\n65\t**rhods-operator** is the platform root: it owns all component CRDs (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and three service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that manage cross-cutting platform infrastructure. Every component operator depends on rhods-operator for activation and lifecycle management.\n66\t\n67\t**kserve** is the most connected serving component with 13 CRDs, 4 independent controller binaries (manager, llmisvc, localmodel, kserve-module), and integration with Gateway API, KEDA, cert-manager, Istio, and the Gateway API Inference Extension. Both rhods-operator and model-registry depend on it.\n68\t\n69\t**odh-model-controller** serves as the serving control plane companion to kserve, managing 10 reconcilers covering InferenceService, InferenceGraph, LLMInferenceService, and Gateway resources. It provides the model-serving-api REST endpoint and manages Kuadrant AuthPolicies and KEDA autoscaling for the serving layer.\n70\t\n71\t**odh-dashboard** is the primary user interface, implementing a micro-frontend architecture with Webpack Module Federation. It watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect real-time platform state.\n72\t\n73\t**models-as-a-service** is the multi-tenant model serving gateway, providing OpenAI-compatible API access with Kuadrant-based authentication and subscription governance.\n74\t\n75\t### Integration Patterns\n76\t\n77\t| Pattern | Components Using | Description |\n78\t|---------|------------------|-------------|\n79\t| CRD-based orchestration | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, models-as-a-service, kuberay, kueue-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, spark-operator, model-registry-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, ai-gateway-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator | Each component defines cluster-scoped CRDs watched by the platform operator; component operators reconcile their own CRDs into namespace-scoped workloads |\n80\t| ODH module operator pattern | kserve (kserve-module), ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, ogx-k8s-operator | Module operators embed odh-platform-utilities framework for standardized manifest rendering, deploy actions, garbage collection, and status reporting |\n81\t| Gateway API routing | rhods-operator, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, kserve (llmisvc), odh-model-controller | HTTPRoute resources reference the data-science-gateway for authenticated external access; components own their routes and bind to a shared gateway |\n82\t| kube-rbac-proxy sidecar | kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator (monitoring proxies), odh-dashboard | TLS-terminating authentication proxy injected as a sidecar; validates tokens via Kubernetes TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview |\n83\t| Conditional CRD watch | kserve, models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator | Controllers check for CRD availability before registering watches, enabling graceful degradation when optional platform features are not installed |\n84\t| Platform TLS profile alignment | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, workbenches-operator, trainer | Operators read the OpenShift APIServer TLS security profile and apply matching cipher suites and minimum TLS version to their own endpoints |\n85\t| Kuadrant policy integration | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator | Kuadrant AuthPolicy and TokenRateLimitPolicy resources provide gateway-level authentication, authorization, and rate limiting |\n86\t\n87\t## Platform Network Architecture\n88\t\n89\t### Namespaces\n90\t\n91\t| Namespace | Purpose | Components |\n92\t|-----------|---------|------------|\n93\t| redhat-ods-operator / opendatahub-operator-system | Operator namespace | rhods-operator |\n94\t| redhat-ods-applications / opendatahub | Platform applications | odh-dashboard, odh-model-controller, kserve controllers, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, notebooks, data-science-pipelines-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, kueue-operator, ai-gateway-operator |\n95\t| openshift-ingress | Gateway infrastructure | data-science-gateway (referenced by HTTPRoutes) |\n96\t| {user-namespaces} | User workloads | InferenceService pods, notebook pods, pipeline runs, training jobs |\n97\t| kserve | KServe controllers | kserve-controller-manager, llmisvc controller, localmodel controllers |\n98\t| models-as-a-service | MaaS namespace | maas-api, maas-controller; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n99\t| kuadrant-system | Kuadrant infrastructure | Authorino/Kuadrant controllers; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n100\t\n101\t### Ingress Integration\n102\t\n103\t| Gateway / Ingress Surface | Owner / Creator | Routing Resources | Data Plane | Authentication Composition | Backend Consumers | Status |\n104\t|---------------------------|----------------|---------------------|------------|----------------------------|-------------------|--------|\n105\t| data-science-gateway (openshift-ingress) | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | HTTPRoutes from models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator, kserve llmisvc | Envoy (via Istio/SAIL or gateway-api controller) | Conditional: EnvoyFilter -> ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy for dashboard; Kuadrant AuthPolicy for MaaS; kube-rbac-proxy sidecar for model-registry | odh-dashboard, models-as-a-service, model-registry, kserve LLMInferenceService | derived |\n106\t| OpenShift Routes (various namespaces) | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator, odh-observability, trustyai-service-operator | Route resources per component | OpenShift HAProxy router | TLS termination at router; kube-rbac-proxy at pod level | odh-dashboard, model-registry REST, Prometheus proxies, TrustyAI service | observed |\n107\t| maas-default-gateway (openshift-ingress) | models-as-a-service (references in HTTPRoute) | HTTPRoute maas-api-route: /v1/models, /v1/subscriptions, /v1/api-keys, /maas-api/* | Envoy (via gateway controller) | Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy: API key + TokenReview + optional OIDC JWT -> OPA authorization | maas-api backend (port 8443) | observed |\n108\t\n109\t### Gateway Inventory\n110\t\n111\t| Gateway | Namespace / Class | Creator / Owner | Route Consumers | Authentication Model | Centrality | Status |\n112\t|---------|------------------|-----------------|----------------|----------------------|------------|--------|\n113\t| data-science-gateway | openshift-ingress / platform GatewayClass | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | odh-dashboard (via kube-auth-proxy), models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, kserve llmisvc, odh-model-controller | Per-route: EnvoyFilter ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy (dashboard), Kuadrant AuthPolicy (MaaS), kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (model-registry) | central/shared | derived |\n114\t| maas-default-gateway | openshift-ingress | models-as-a-service deployment manifest | maas-api | Kuadrant/Authorino: API key extraction (sk-oai-*) + K8s TokenReview + optional OIDC JWT; OPA Rego authorization for subscription/group/binding | capability-specific | observed |\n115\t\n116\t### Ingress Analysis\n117\t\n118\tRHOAI 3.6-ea.1 presents a multi-generation ingress architecture. The primary ingress path uses Gateway API through the `data-science-gateway`, provisioned by the rhods-operator Gateway service controller. This gateway serves as the central platform ingress node, with multiple independently owned components attaching HTTPRoutes to it. The rhods-operator creates the GatewayClass and Gateway resources, and conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically owning EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected on the cluster.\n119\t\n120\tA parallel ingress path through OpenShift Routes persists for backward compatibility and for components that have not yet migrated to Gateway API. The model-registry-operator explicitly implements dual ingress, creating both HTTPRoutes (referencing data-science-gateway) and OpenShift Routes, with the backend port selection conditionally routing through kube-rbac-proxy when configured.\n121\t\n122\tThe Models-as-a-Service subsystem introduces a third ingress pattern: it references a `maas-default-gateway` in its HTTPRoute manifest and layers Kuadrant/Authorino authentication at the gateway level. The MaaSAuthPolicyReconciler programmatically constructs AuthPolicy resources with three authentication methods (API key, TokenReview, OIDC JWT) and OPA-based authorization. This is architecturally distinct from the dashboard's kube-auth-proxy OIDC pattern and from the pod-level kube-rbac-proxy pattern used by model-registry.\n123\t\n124\tThe EnvoyFilter -> ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy chain for dashboard access is conditionally created by the rhods-operator Gateway controller when Istio CRDs are present. The specific filter configuration (selector, target service/port, auth-check path, route scope) is dynamically generated by the controller. Source inspection confirms that the Gateway controller owns EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources and deploys a kube-auth-proxy with OIDC support, but the exact ext_authz filter wiring details are rendered at reconciliation time from Go code rather than static manifests, making the full chain `derived` rather than `observed`. [source: rhods-operator internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:42-103]\n125\t\n126\tThe relationship between data-science-gateway and maas-default-gateway is unresolved from the structured inventory alone. Both are referenced in the `openshift-ingress` namespace, and they may be the same Gateway resource with different names used by different components, or they may be distinct Gateway resources. The models-as-a-service HTTPRoute explicitly references `maas-default-gateway` as its parentRef.\n127\t\n128\t### Gateway Authentication\n129\t\n130\t| Gateway / Surface | Identity or Credential Input | Gateway Policy | Envoy / Istio Filters | Auth Service or Endpoint | Backend / Application Auth | Failure or Uncertainty |\n131\t|------------------|-----------------------------|----------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|---------------------------|------------------------|\n132\t| data-science-gateway (dashboard path) | OAuth2/OIDC token, OpenShift OAuth cookie | None observed at Gateway level | EnvoyFilter ext_authz (conditional, when Istio CRDs present) | kube-auth-proxy on port 4180: /oauth2/auth endpoint, OAuth2/OIDC + K8s TokenReview + Basic Auth | kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on dashboard pod (port 8443) -> Node.js backend; BFF sidecars perform independent TokenReview + SubjectAccessReview | EnvoyFilter configuration is rendered dynamically; exact selector, route scope, and conditional enablement are derived from Go controller code |\n133\t| maas-default-gateway (MaaS API) | API key (Bearer sk-oai-*), K8s ServiceAccount token, OIDC JWT (optional) | Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy | None observed (Kuadrant operates at policy level) | Authorino: API key callback to maas-api /internal/v1/api-keys/validate; K8s TokenReview; OIDC JWT validation | maas-api TenantAuthMiddleware: TokenReview + SubjectAccessReview for /v1/tenants (internal-only path) | OPA Rego authorization rules for subscription/group/binding; /v1/tenants intentionally excluded from Gateway routing |\n134\t| OpenShift Routes (model-registry) | Bearer token | None (OpenShift router handles TLS termination only) | None | None at route level | kube-rbac-proxy sidecar: SubjectAccessReview for Service get permission in target namespace | kube-rbac-proxy deployment is conditional on operator configuration |\n135\t| OpenShift Routes (monitoring) | Bearer token | None | None | None at route level | kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on Prometheus proxies: cluster-monitoring-view ClusterRole | All monitoring Routes use TLS |\n136\t\n137\t### Internal Service Mesh\n138\t\n139\t| Setting | Value | Components Using |\n140\t|---------|-------|------------------|\n141\t| Istio integration | Conditional (when CRDs detected) | rhods-operator (EnvoyFilter, DestinationRule), kserve llmisvc (DestinationRule), odh-model-controller (EnvoyFilter) |\n142\t| mTLS | STRICT (when Istio present, for llm-d ModelExpress gRPC control plane) | llm-d (documented in security manifests) |\n143\t| Gateway API | Primary ingress mechanism | rhods-operator, kserve, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator |\n144\t| Kuadrant | API authentication and rate limiting | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator |\n145\t| cert-manager | TLS certificate automation | kserve, kuberay, kueue-operator, spark-operator, workbenches-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator, openshell |\n146\t\n147\t## Platform Security\n148\t\n149\t### Authentication Mechanisms\n150\t\n151\t| Pattern | Components Using | Enforcement Point |\n152\t|---------|------------------|-------------------|\n153\t| kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (SubjectAccessReview) | kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator (monitoring proxies), odh-dashboard, odh-observability | Pod-level TLS-terminating proxy; validates tokens via K8s TokenReview and checks authorization via SubjectAccessReview |\n154\t| Kubernetes TokenReview API | kube-auth-proxy, kserve (router), models-as-a-service (maas-api), odh-dashboard (BFF sidecars) | Application-level; validates Bearer tokens against kube-apiserver |\n155\t| OAuth2/OIDC (via kube-auth-proxy) | odh-dashboard (via rhods-operator Gateway controller) | Gateway-level; kube-auth-proxy handles OAuth2 flow, session management, and token validation |\n156\t| Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator | Gateway-level; API key, TokenReview, and OIDC JWT authentication with OPA authorization |\n157\t| SubjectAccessReview | odh-dashboard (BFF sidecars), models-as-a-service (TenantAuthMiddleware), odh-model-controller (gateway discoverer) | Application-level; fine-grained authorization checks against K8s RBAC |\n158\t| Admission webhook TLS (service-ca) | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, models-as-a-service, workbenches-operator, odh-dashboard, kueue-operator, kuberay, spark-operator, trainer, training-operator | Webhook endpoints; TLS certificates provisioned by OpenShift service-CA operator |\n159\t\n160\t### Container Security\n161\t\n162\t| Pattern | Components Using | Description |\n163\t|---------|------------------|-------------|\n164\t| FIPS-compliant build (strictfipsruntime) | rhods-operator, kserve (kserve-module), kube-auth-proxy, models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard, model-registry-operator, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, spark-operator, kueue-operator, kuberay, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator | CGO_ENABLED=1 + GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime routes Go crypto through system OpenSSL |\n165\t| UBI 9 base images | All shipped Go components | Red Hat Universal Base Image 9 provides FIPS-validated OpenSSL and security updates |\n166\t| Platform TLS profile alignment | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, workbenches-operator, trainer | Reads OpenShift APIServer TLS security profile; falls back to TLS 1.2 + Mozilla Intermediate ciphers |\n167\t| Non-root execution | MLServer, kserve Python model servers, data-science-pipelines | Container images run as non-root user (typically UID 1001) |\n168\t\n169\t## Platform Admission Webhooks\n170\t\n171\t### Webhook Ownership\n172\t\n173\t| Component | Mutating | Validating | Conversion | Failure Policy |\n174\t|-----------|----------|------------|------------|----------------|\n175\t| rhods-operator | 3 | 6 | 1 | Fail |\n176\t| kserve | 4 | 11 | 1 | Fail |\n177\t| odh-model-controller | 10 | 6 | 0 | Fail |\n178\t| kueue-operator | 34 | 36 | 0 | Fail |\n179\t| kuberay | 2 | 6 | 0 | Fail |\n180\t| spark-operator | 8 | 6 | 1 | Fail |\n181\t| odh-dashboard | 0 | 4 | 1 | Fail |\n182\t| models-as-a-service | 0 | 4 | 0 | Fail |\n183\t| workbenches-operator | 4 | 0 | 1 | Fail |\n184\t| model-registry-operator | 2 | 2 | 1 | Fail |\n185\t| data-science-pipelines | 1 | 1 | 0 | Fail |\n186\t| kubeflow | 2 | 2 | 1 | Fail |\n187\t| trainer | 1 | 3 | 0 | Fail |\n188\t| training-operator | 0 | 5 | 0 | Fail |\n189\t| trustyai-service-operator | 0 | 0 | 1 | Fail |\n190\t| ogx-k8s-operator | 0 | 2 | 0 | Fail |\n191\t| odh-observability | 3 | 0 | 0 | Fail |\n192\t\n193\t### Cross-Component Targets\n194\t\n195\t| Source Component | Webhook | Target Component | Intercepted Types | Type | Failure Policy |\n196\t|------------------|---------|------------------|--------------------|------|----------------|\n197\t| rhods-operator | podmonitor-injector | prometheus-operator | podmonitors (monitoring.coreos.com) | mutating | Fail |\n198\t| rhods-operator | dashboard-hardwareprofile-validator | workbenches-operator | hardwareprofiles (infrastructure.opendatahub.io) | validating | Fail |\n199\t| rhods-operator | dashboard-acceleratorprofile-validator | odh-dashboard | acceleratorprofiles | validating | Fail |\n200\t| odh-model-controller | (InferenceService webhooks) | kserve | inferenceservices, servingruntimes (serving.kserve.io) | mutating + validating | Fail |\n201\t| kserve | inferenceservice.kserve-webhook-server.pod-mutator | (all pods in serving namespaces) | pods | mutating | Fail |\n202\t| workbenches-operator | hardware-profile-injector, connection-notebook | kubeflow/notebooks | notebooks (kubeflow.org) | mutating | Fail |\n203\t\n204\t### Cross-Cutting Concerns\n205\t\n206\t| Concern | Components | Affected Resources | Pattern |\n207\t|---------|------------|--------------------|---------|\n208\t| InferenceService lifecycle | kserve, odh-model-controller | inferenceservices.serving.kserve.io | Expected layering: kserve validates/defaults core CRD semantics; odh-model-controller adds platform-specific mutations (auth, monitoring, networking) |\n209\t| Hardware profile injection | rhods-operator, workbenches-operator | hardwareprofiles (infrastructure.opendatahub.io), notebooks (kubeflow.org) | Expected layering: rhods-operator validates HardwareProfile CRDs; workbenches-operator injects resolved profiles into notebook pods |\n210\t| Pod mutation for serving | kserve | pods | KServe injects sidecar containers into inference pods for storage initialization and model agent |\n211\t| Monitoring injection | rhods-operator | podmonitors (monitoring.coreos.com) | Platform operator injects monitoring labels into PodMonitor resources |\n212\t\n213\t### Overlay Deployment\n214\t\n215\tOverlay membership data is not available for this generation. Webhook entries reflect manifest-level inventory only.\n216\t\n217\t## Data Flows\n218\t\n219\t### Key Platform Workflows\n220\t\n221\t#### Workflow 1: Model Deployment via Dashboard\n222\t\n223\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n224\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n225\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User creates InferenceService/LLMInferenceService via UI; BFF sidecar validates via SubjectAccessReview | kube-apiserver |\n226\t| 2 | kube-apiserver | Admission webhooks validate and default the CRD resource | kserve, odh-model-controller |\n227\t| 3 | kserve (manager or llmisvc) | Reconciles InferenceService: creates Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute/Route, HPA/KEDA ScaledObject | Kubernetes API |\n228\t| 4 | odh-model-controller | Reconciles InferenceService: adds NetworkPolicy, ServiceMonitor, PodMonitor, Route, Kuadrant AuthPolicy | Kubernetes API |\n229\t| 5 | Gateway controller | HTTPRoute attaches to data-science-gateway; external traffic routed through Envoy to model pod | Model server pod |\n230\t\n231\t#### Workflow 2: Models-as-a-Service API Request\n232\t\n233\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n234\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n235\t| 1 | External client | Sends request with API key/token to maas-default-gateway | Gateway (Envoy) |\n236\t| 2 | Kuadrant/Authorino | AuthPolicy validates: API key callback to maas-api, TokenReview, optional OIDC JWT; OPA authorizes subscription/group | maas-api |\n237\t| 3 | maas-api | Processes model listing, subscription management, or API key lifecycle; queries PostgreSQL for tenant state | PostgreSQL |\n238\t| 4 | maas-controller | MaaSModelRefReconciler watches KServe LLMInferenceService for model availability; creates HTTPRoutes for model endpoints | kserve |\n239\t\n240\t#### Workflow 3: Notebook Workspace Creation\n241\t\n242\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n243\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n244\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User creates Notebook resource via notebooks UI module | kube-apiserver |\n245\t| 2 | workbenches-operator | Mutating webhook injects HardwareProfile resources and data-connection secrets into notebook pod template | kube-apiserver |\n246\t| 3 | kubeflow (notebook-controller) | Reconciles Notebook resource: creates StatefulSet, Service, PodDisruptionBudget | Kubernetes API |\n247\t| 4 | rhods-operator | Gateway controller creates Route for notebook access via kube-auth-proxy OIDC authentication | OpenShift router |\n248\t\n249\t#### Workflow 4: Pipeline Execution\n250\t\n251\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n252\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n253\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User submits pipeline run via pipelines UI module | data-science-pipelines API server |\n254\t| 2 | data-science-pipelines-operator | Manages DSPA resources: deploys API server, persistence agent, scheduled workflow controller | Kubernetes API |\n255\t| 3 | data-science-pipelines | API server creates Argo Workflow; scheduled workflow controller manages recurring runs | argo-workflows |\n256\t| 4 | argo-workflows | Executes workflow steps as pods; artifact storage via SeaweedFS/S3 | Pipeline component pods |\n257\t\n258\t#### Workflow 5: LLM Disaggregated Inference (llm-d)\n259\t\n260\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n261\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n262\t| 1 | kserve (llmisvc) | Reconciles LLMInferenceService: creates prefill and decode Deployments, InferencePool, Gateway resources | Kubernetes API |\n263\t| 2 | llm-d-router (EPP) | Endpoint Picker Plugin selects backend pods through Gateway API InferencePool; model-aware routing via Envoy sidecar | llm-d decode/prefill pods |\n264\t| 3 | llm-d (decode pods) | vLLM OpenAI-compatible API server serves inference requests; KV cache coordination via ModelExpress gRPC | llm-d-kv-cache |\n265\t| 4 | workload-variant-autoscaler | Monitors InferencePool metrics via Prometheus; scales decode/prefill replicas via KEDA | Kubernetes API |\n266\t\n267\t## Deployment Architecture\n268\t\n269\t### Deployment Topology\n270\t\n271\tThe platform deploys across four namespace tiers. The operator namespace (redhat-ods-operator or opendatahub-operator-system) hosts rhods-operator and the platform-level admission webhooks. The applications namespace (redhat-ods-applications or opendatahub) hosts the dashboard, model controllers, and module operators. Per-component namespaces (kserve, models-as-a-service) isolate specific subsystems. User namespaces host workloads: inference service pods, notebook StatefulSets, pipeline workflow pods, training jobs, and Ray clusters.\n272\t\n273\tThe operator tier manages the control plane: rhods-operator reconciles DataScienceCluster and DSCInitialization CRs, triggering per-component operators to deploy their workloads. Module operators (kserve-module, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, etc.) each run as separate processes with dedicated RBAC identities and leader election, following the ODH module operator pattern for standardized manifest rendering and lifecycle management.\n274\t\n275\tGateway infrastructure is deployed in the openshift-ingress namespace, where the data-science-gateway receives HTTPRoutes from multiple components. Monitoring infrastructure (Prometheus collectors and proxies with kube-rbac-proxy sidecars) is deployed in the applications namespace by the rhods-operator Monitoring service controller.\n276\t\n277\t### Resource Summary\n278\t\n279\tThe platform comprises 72 analyzed components including: 19+ operators/controllers (rhods-operator, kserve x4, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard-operator, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, kuberay, kueue-operator, spark-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator), 145 CRDs, 210 admission webhooks, 122 services, and 1658 RBAC entries across all components.\n280\t\n281\t### Deployment Manifest Patterns\n282\t\n283\t#### Kustomize Patterns\n284\t\n285\t| Pattern | Components Using | Description |\n286\t|---------|------------------|-------------|\n287\t| Distribution overlays (odh/rhoai) | kserve, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard, rhods-operator, model-registry-operator | Separate kustomize overlays for ODH and RHOAI distributions with image replacements, patch additions, and RBAC modifications |\n288\t| Template-based rendering | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator | Go template manifests rendered at reconciliation time with runtime values (namespace, image references, configuration) |\n289\t| Manifest bundling in container image | trainer-operator, workbenches-operator | Manifests copied into operator container at build time from /opt/manifests-template/ directories |\n290\t| Server-side apply with field ownership | trainer-operator, workbenches-operator | Framework deploy action applies resources with component-specific field manager names, enabling precise garbage collection |\n291\t\n292\t#### Distribution Variants\n293\t\n294\t| Component | ODH Path | RHOAI Path | Key Differences |\n295\t|-----------|----------|------------|-----------------|\n296\t| kserve | config/overlays/odh/ | config/overlays/rhoai/ (downstream builds) | ODH removes upstream kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, replaces with odh-kube-auth-proxy; RHOAI adds FIPS build flags |\n297\t| odh-dashboard | manifests/odh/ | manifests/rhoai/ | Federation configuration differs (module enablement); RHOAI adds additional BFF sidecars |\n298\t| rhods-operator | config/overlays/odh/ | config/overlays/rhoai/ | Component manifests differ; RHOAI includes additional component CRDs |\n299\t\n300\t#### Manifest Consumption Pipeline\n301\t\n302\tComponent manifests flow from upstream open-source repositories through midstream integration branches (opendatahub-io organization) to downstream distribution branches (red-hat-data-services organization). The auto_merge sync mechanism maintains branch alignment. rhods-operator's get_all_manifests.sh script (or equivalent pipeline tooling) aggregates component manifests into the operator repository, where they are rendered and deployed to the cluster during reconciliation. Konflux build pipelines produce container images with FIPS-compliant build flags and UBI base images.\n303\t\n304\t### High Availability\n305\t\n306\t| Component | Replicas | HA Mechanism |\n307\t|-----------|----------|--------------|\n308\t| rhods-operator | 1 | Leader election via controller-runtime |\n309\t| kserve-controller-manager | 1 | Leader election via ConfigMap/Lease |\n310\t| kserve llmisvc controller | 1 | Leader election via Lease |\n311\t| kserve-module controller | 1 | Leader election via Lease |\n312\t| odh-model-controller | 1 | Leader election via controller-runtime |\n313\t| odh-dashboard | Configurable | Stateless replicas; session state in Redis/Valkey for kube-auth-proxy multi-replica |\n314\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api) | Configurable | Stateless API server; state in PostgreSQL |\n315\t| kueue-operator | 1 | Leader election |\n316\t| Model serving pods | Configurable | HPA, KEDA ScaledObjects, LeaderWorkerSets for multi-node inference |\n317\t\n318\t### Disconnected Support\n319\t\n320\tDisconnected deployment support is unresolved across all 66 components that lack explicit disconnected_deployment evidence. The platform is expected to support air-gapped deployments through the OLM `RELATED_IMAGE` pattern and oc-mirror for image mirroring, but no complete deterministic evidence family was extracted from the analyzed component sources for this generation. Components that reference container images by digest (via Konflux build pipelines) are compatible with mirrored registries, but the specific image-override and proxy configuration mechanisms were not confirmed from the structured inventory.\n321\t\n322\t### Multi-Architecture Support\n323\t\n324\t| Architecture | Support Level | Notes |\n325\t|--------------|---------------|-------|\n326\t| amd64 | Full | Primary build and test architecture for all components |\n327\t| arm64 | Partial | Some upstream components support arm64; RHOAI distribution support unresolved |\n328\t| ppc64le | Unresolved | No evidence of ppc64le builds in analyzed components |\n329\t| s390x | Unresolved | No evidence of s390x builds in analyzed components |\n330\t\n331\t## Platform Maturity\n332\t\n333\t- **Total Components**: 72\n334\t- **Operator-based Components**: 19+\n335\t- **Gateway API Coverage**: Primary ingress mechanism; dual-path with OpenShift Routes for backward compatibility\n336\t- **mTLS Enforcement**: Conditional (STRICT when Istio/SAIL present for llm-d control plane; platform-wide mTLS depends on Istio deployment)\n337\t- **CRD API Versions**: v1alpha1 (majority), v1alpha2 (LLMInferenceService), v1beta1 (InferenceService), v1 (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, HardwareProfile), v2 (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization)\n338\t- **FIPS Compliance**: All shipped Go binaries built with strictfipsruntime; Python components inherit FIPS from UBI base OpenSSL\n339\t- **Disconnected Support**: Unresolved (expected via OLM RELATED_IMAGE pattern but not confirmed from source)\n340\t- **Multi-Architecture**: amd64 (full); arm64, ppc64le, s390x (unresolved)\n341\t\n342\t## Platform Architectural Analysis\n343\t\n344\tRHOAI 3.6-ea.1 represents a platform in active architectural evolution across several dimensions. The most significant patterns and tensions observed across all 72 component analyses are:\n345\t\n346\t**Module operator proliferation.** The platform has adopted a module operator pattern (via odh-platform-utilities framework) where each major capability runs its own operator process with independent RBAC, leader election, and lifecycle management. This provides clean separation of concerns and independent release cadences but results in a large number of operator pods (19+ controllers), each with substantial RBAC footprints. The ai-gateway-operator alone has 128 RBAC entries spanning 10+ API groups. This proliferation creates operational complexity in monitoring, debugging, and resource consumption.\n347\t\n348\t**Authentication fragmentation.** The platform uses at least four distinct authentication patterns: (1) kube-auth-proxy OIDC at the gateway level for dashboard access, (2) Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy for MaaS API access with API keys, TokenReview, and OIDC, (3) kube-rbac-proxy sidecars for pod-level SubjectAccessReview enforcement on metrics and API endpoints, and (4) application-level TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview in component code. While each pattern serves a valid use case, the lack of a unified authentication architecture means that different capabilities present different credential requirements and failure modes to users and operators.\n349\t\n350\t**Gateway API transition.** The platform is mid-migration from OpenShift Routes to Gateway API. The rhods-operator provisions a central data-science-gateway, but not all components have migrated: model-registry-operator maintains dual ingress, some components still use Routes exclusively, and models-as-a-service references a separately-named gateway (maas-default-gateway). The conditional Istio integration (EnvoyFilter/DestinationRule created only when Istio CRDs are detected) adds complexity to the ingress story, as the authentication chain differs between Istio-present and Istio-absent clusters.\n351\t\n352\t**KServe multi-controller complexity.** KServe ships 4 independent controller binaries (manager, llmisvc, localmodel/localmodelnode) plus the kserve-module operator, totaling 5 processes with separate RBAC identities. The llmisvc controller alone manages 13 resource types including HPA, KEDA ScaledObjects, LeaderWorkerSets, InferencePools, and Gateway resources. This reflects the rapid feature expansion of the LLM serving stack (disaggregated inference, multi-node serving, local model caching) but creates a large blast radius for any KServe upgrade.\n353\t\n354\t**Shared infrastructure dependencies.** Multiple components independently depend on cert-manager, prometheus-operator, and Gateway API, each implementing their own conditional watch patterns. This means the platform has soft dependencies on these infrastructure components without a centralized dependency management mechanism — each component independently checks for CRD availability and degrades independently.\n355\t\n356\t**Supply chain consistency.** All analyzed Go components use the FIPS-compliant build pattern (CGO_ENABLED=1 + GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime) on UBI 9 base images, providing strong build-time FIPS posture. However, the upstream KServe core manager Dockerfile uses CGO_ENABLED=0 (static linking, not FIPS-compliant), relying on the Konflux build pipeline to override these flags for downstream builds. Python components (KServe model servers, data-science-pipelines) inherit FIPS from the UBI base OpenSSL but have no explicit FIPS opt-in in their application code.\n357\t\n358\t**Unresolved cross-cutting topics.** High availability, disconnected deployment, and supply chain evidence remain unresolved across the majority of components. While the platform architecture supports HA through leader election (operators) and horizontal scaling (workloads), no deterministic evidence of replica configuration, failover behavior, or disruption budgets was extracted at the platform level. Disconnected deployment support is architecturally expected through OLM and oc-mirror but was not confirmed from component source analysis.\n359\t\n360\t### Serving Path Evolution\n361\t\n362\t| Path | Evidence Components | Entry/Routing Surface | Runtime or Provider Role | Source |\n363\t|------|---------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|--------|\n364\t| KServe InferenceService (single-model) | kserve (manager), odh-model-controller | Gateway API HTTPRoute or OpenShift Route -> model server pod | vLLM, OpenVINO, MLServer, HuggingFace, sklearn, XGBoost, LightGBM, TorchServe, Triton model servers via ClusterServingRuntime | kserve config/runtimes/, pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/controller.go |\n365\t| KServe LLMInferenceService (llm-d disaggregated) | kserve (llmisvc), llm-d, llm-d-router, llm-d-kv-cache, llm-d-routing-sidecar, workload-variant-autoscaler | Gateway API -> InferencePool -> EPP (llm-d-router) -> Envoy sidecar -> prefill/decode pods | vLLM with disaggregated prefill/decode; ModelExpress KV cache broker; KEDA/WVA autoscaling | kserve pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/controller.go, llm-d guides/recipes/ |\n366\t| KServe InferenceGraph (DAG routing) | kserve (manager, router) | Gateway API or Route -> kserve router binary | Router authenticates via K8s TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview, fans out to model nodes per graph topology | kserve cmd/router/main.go |\n367\t| Models-as-a-Service (multi-tenant gateway) | models-as-a-service, kserve (via LLMInferenceService watch) | maas-default-gateway HTTPRoute -> Kuadrant AuthPolicy -> maas-api | OpenAI-compatible REST API; subscription governance; delegates to KServe LLMInferenceService backends | models-as-a-service deployment/base/maas-api/networking/httproute.yaml |\n368\t| Model Registry serving | model-registry, model-registry-operator | Gateway API HTTPRoute (data-science-gateway) or OpenShift Route -> kube-rbac-proxy -> model-registry REST API | Model metadata storage and retrieval; PostgreSQL backend | model-registry-operator internal/controller/config/templates/ |\n369\t\n370\t## Version-Specific Changes (3.6-ea.1)\n371\t\n372\t| Component | Changes |\n373\t|-----------|---------|\n374\t| rhods-operator | Active development: latest component image syncs (odh-dashboard, cluster-validator); auto-merge from upstream opendatahub-operator |\n375\t| kserve | Upstream main merge; Konflux pipeline sync; gatekeeper workflow additions |\n376\t| models-as-a-service | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow; UBI toolset digest updates |\n377\t| kube-auth-proxy | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow; UBI toolset digest updates |\n378\t| odh-model-controller | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow additions |\n379\t| ai-gateway-operator | Upstream stable merge |\n380\t| model-registry-operator | Upstream stable merge |\n381\t| workbenches-operator | Upstream v1.x merge |\n382\t| trainer-operator | Upstream main merge |\n383\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bdbe1c6b5d9e58e8","agent_id":"a1e7319239208d111","session_id":"63a747e2-9d8d-4636-a22b-5d195afcf157","uuid":"aae5678d-a