er: PM /\n   Observability team): the answer changes the *content* of the convention guideline but\n   not which epics exist or their structure — the P0/P1 required field set is fixed by the\n   HLRs. Recorded as an open design decision on the documentation epic (reflected in its\n   open-questions signal); the guideline is scoped to the three required fields.\n3. **Open question — 75% embedded-identity figure unverified:** the audit is the first\n   step of the manager-binary implementation epic. The conversion proceeds the same way\n   regardless of the actual percentage (failure = fix-and-retry), so no Investigation epic.\n   **Re-split trigger:** if the audit finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the\n   ~75% estimate implies, re-split the manager epic by controller tier.\n4. **Open question — cloudmanager release timing** (owner: PM / ODH Operator team): the\n   cloudmanager epic is independently schedulable and may follow in a subsequent release\n   (cloudmanager deploys only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes: AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave).\n   This is a scheduling decision, not a structural one; the epics remain parallel.\n5. **Open question — which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns** (owner:\n   SRE / Support): resolved during the log-consumer regression validation that is an\n   acceptance criterion on both implementation epics; not an Investigation.\n6. **External COO adoption is voluntary** per platform policy (overlay 0008): no epic is\n   created for the Cluster Observability Operator team — the strategy's deliverable is\n   publishing the convention and filing tracking issues, which is scoped to the\n   documentation epic. Adoption timing is an externally controlled risk, not a dependency.\n7. **Epic size:** the manager-binary epic is at the upper end of the ~2-week guideline\n   (audit ~0.5 sprint + conversion ~0.75–1 sprint per the strategy estimate). It is kept\n   as one epic because it is a single mechanical sweep over one binary's log call sites —\n   splitting by controller tier would duplicate the audit, the test logger harness, and\n   the lint wiring. Re-split trigger: controller tier, if the audit exceeds the ~75%\n   estimate (see warning 3).\n8. **Component mapping (non-obvious):** the strategy's affected component `rhods-operator`\n   (ODH Operator team) has no verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. All epics\n   are assigned the closest canonical name **`OpenShift AI Productization`** — the RHAI\n   component covering productization of the RHOAI platform, of which rhods-operator (the\n   platform root operator, upstream opendatahub-operator) is the primary artifact. All\n   three epics span the single strategy component; the manager/cloudmanager split is a\n   binary-level architectural boundary within it.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. There is no external dependency gating delivery of user-facing value: the\ncontroller-runtime/logr foundation already exists (internal dependency, status: exists),\nand the only external dependency (COO team adoption of the convention) is voluntary,\nout-of-scope per platform policy, and does not gate any epic. The cloudmanager epic may\ntrail the manager epic in release timing (see Health Warning 4) without any DAG change.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":55752,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bdbe1c6b5d9e58e8","agent_id":"a1e7319239208d111","session_id":"63a747e2-9d8d-4636-a22b-5d195afcf157","uuid":"0ee29a70-ea0d-441e-a32e-a346e1046513","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:40:24.328Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
