{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d51386be-0797-457c-a77a-99dd9be4095a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a954f2b53b1f190e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-y1gfxeze/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Structured log field convention documentation and external publication\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic authors and publishes the documentation deliverable of the strategy: a\ncontribution guideline specifying the structured log field convention for rhods-operator\ncontrollers (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, camelCase naming), explaining the\nthree-problem diagnosis that motivated it, and providing before/after code examples drawn\nfrom the completed conversion work in both rhods-operator binaries. It also publishes the\nconvention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability\nOperator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) per platform policy (overlay 0008 — RHOAI does\nnot install, manage, or control external operator dependencies), and documents the DSCI\nkey-name migration for operators with existing log queries. This is substantive content\nauthoring, distinct from the code conversion, and follows the Red Hat docs content\nprocess.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline:\n\n- The structured log field convention: required fields (`name`, `namespace`,\n  `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's\n  existing structured log key conventions), and guidance for cluster-scoped resources\n  (namespace omitted or empty).\n- The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, and\n  missing `resourceKind`.\n- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded resource identity to\n  structured KV pairs, taken from the converted manager and cloudmanager controller code.\n- A DSCI key-name migration guide (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` →\n  `name`/`namespace`) with before/after Loki/CloudWatch query examples, documented for\n  release notes so the transition happens in a single release.\n- The convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development in\n  rhods-operator (what a new controller's error logs must emit and how the CI lint check\n  enforces it).\n- Publication of the convention for external operator adoption (Loki Operator, Tempo\n  Operator) as a specification for voluntary adoption, plus tracking issues filed in the\n  COO projects.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The contribution guideline specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`,\n  `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis\n  (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing `resourceKind`) — measured\n  by review against the strategy's documentation acceptance criterion.\n- The DSCI key migration is documented with before/after query examples suitable for\n  release notes — measured by the migration guide covering both old and new field names.\n- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implementation: a reviewer confirms\n  the before/after examples match the converted log calls actually shipped in the\n  rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n- The convention is published in a form external operator teams (COO: Loki Operator,\n  Tempo Operator) can adopt, and tracking issues are filed in the COO projects — measured\n  by the published specification and the filed issues referencing it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-8 (P1): document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller\n  development and publish for external operator adoption.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64913,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bdbe1c6b5d9e58e8","agent_id":"a1e7319239208d111","session_id":"63a747e2-9d8d-4