{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5993540aebf3037","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Outcome\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold and not docs-only. Although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single\n17\tcomponent (rhods-operator), and is owned by a single active team (ODH Operator team), the\n18\tscope spans architecturally distinct sub-systems and work-product types that warrant\n19\tseparate PR review cycles:\n20\t\n21\t- The **manager binary** and the **cloudmanager binary** are two separate Go executables\n22\t  with disjoint controller sets (manager: DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller\n23\t  with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers, 15 action files; cloudmanager: 3\n24\t  cloud-engine controllers on the shared reconciler). They are converted independently.\n25\t- **Code implementation** (log call conversion, unit tests, CI lint enforcement) and\n26\t  **substantive content authoring** (a new contribution guideline documenting the\n27\t  convention, camelCase rationale, three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, and the\n28\t  DSCI key migration guide, published for external operator adoption) are different kinds\n29\t  of work with different review criteria.\n30\t\n31\tNo Investigation epics were created. All four open questions were evaluated against the\n32\tdecision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\") and\n33\tnone qualifies — see Health Warnings.\n34\t\n35\t## Epic List\n36\t\n37\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n38\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Structured log field convention documentation and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n42\t\n43\t## Dependency DAG\n44\t\n45\t```mermaid\n46\tgraph TD\n47\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E001<br/>Manager binary log conversion\"]\n48\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E002<br/>Cloudmanager binary log conversion\"]\n49\t    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E003<br/>Convention documentation\"]\n50\t    E001 --> E003\n51\t    E002 --> E003\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t## DAG Justification\n55\t\n56\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n57\t|------|------|-----------|\n58\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repository, different areas: the manager and cloudmanager binaries have disjoint controller sets and disjoint log call sites. Parallel — merge conflicts are a coordination risk, not a dependency. The cloudmanager release-timing open question reinforces independent schedulability. |\n59\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; the contribution guideline documents the convention as actually implemented, including before/after examples taken from the converted code. |\n60\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same — the guideline also covers the cloudmanager controllers' structured fields. |\n61\t\n62\tCritical path length: 2 (either implementation epic → documentation epic).\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n65\t\n66\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n67\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n68\t| HLR-1: Convert message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Spans both binaries; each epic converts the call sites in its own binary. |\n69\t| HLR-2: DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller. |\n70\t| HLR-3: Service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Service controllers are manager-binary. |\n71\t| HLR-4: All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (15 files, 4 tiers) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | All 15 action files live in the manager binary's controller tree. |\n72\t| HLR-5: Cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager controllers already get `name`/`namespace` from the shared reconciler's request logger; this epic adds `resourceKind` and converts embedded identity. |\n73\t| HLR-6: Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Module controller is a manager-binary tier. |\n74\t| HLR-7: DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 (bundled) | Bundled: `resourceKind` is added in the same edit of the same `log.Error` call sites being converted for HLR-1 — the DSC and component controllers are explicitly in HLR-1's conversion scope. A separate epic would force a second pass over identical call sites and duplicate the audit, tests, and lint wiring; not separately schedulable. |\n75\t| HLR-8: Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | docs-authoring epic; priority P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority: Major → P1). |\n76\t\n77\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n78\t\n79\t## Health Warnings\n80\t\n81\t1. **Pending process gates (non-blocking):** all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture\n82\t   Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\". These\n83\t   are generic strategy-level gates; their outcomes do not change which epics exist or\n84\t   what they do. Flagged for human verification before implementation starts.\n85\t2. **Open question — additional fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (owner: PM /\n86\t   Observability team): the answer changes the *content* of the convention guideline but\n87\t   not which epics exist or their structure — the P0/P1 required field set is fixed by the\n88\t   HLRs. Recorded as an open design decision on the documentation epic (reflected in its\n89\t   open-questions signal); the guideline is scoped to the three required fields.\n90\t3. **Open question — 75% embedded-identity figure unverified:** the audit is the first\n91\t   step of the manager-binary implementation epic. The conversion proceeds the same way\n92\t   regardless of the actual percentage (failure = fix-and-retry), so no Investigation epic.\n93\t   **Re-split trigger:** if the audit finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the\n94\t   ~75% estimate implies, re-split the manager epic by controller tier.\n95\t4. **Open question — cloudmanager release timing** (owner: PM / ODH Operator team): the\n96\t   cloudmanager epic is independently schedulable and may follow in a subsequent release\n97\t   (cloudmanager deploys only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes: AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave).\n98\t   This is a scheduling decision, not a structural one; the epics remain parallel.\n99\t5. **Open question — which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns** (owner:\n100\t   SRE / Support): resolved during the log-consumer regression validation that is an\n101\t   acceptance criterion on both implementation epics; not an Investigation.\n102\t6. **External COO adoption is voluntary** per platform policy (overlay 0008): no epic is\n103\t   created for the Cluster Observability Operator team — the strategy's deliverable is\n104\t   publishing the convention and filing tracking issues, which is scoped to the\n105\t   documentation epic. Adoption timing is an externally controlled risk, not a dependency.\n106\t7. **Epic size:** the manager-binary epic is at the upper end of the ~2-week guideline\n107\t   (audit ~0.5 sprint + conversion ~0.75–1 sprint per the strategy estimate). It is kept\n108\t   as one epic because it is a single mechanical sweep over one binary's log call sites —\n109\t   splitting by controller tier would duplicate the audit, the test logger harness, and\n110\t   the lint wiring. Re-split trigger: controller tier, if the audit exceeds the ~75%\n111\t   estimate (see warning 3).\n112\t8. **Component mapping (non-obvious):** the strategy's affected component `rhods-operator`\n113\t   (ODH Operator team) has no verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. All epics\n114\t   are assigned the closest canonical name **`OpenShift AI Productization`** — the RHAI\n115\t   component covering productization of the RHOAI platform, of which rhods-operator (the\n116\t   platform root operator, upstream opendatahub-operator) is the primary artifact. All\n117\t   three epics span the single strategy component; the manager/cloudmanager split is a\n118\t   binary-level architectural boundary within it.\n119\t\n120\t## Tiered Delivery\n121\t\n122\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency gating delivery of user-facing value: the\n123\tcontroller-runtime/logr foundation already exists (internal dependency, status: exists),\n124\tand the only external dependency (COO team adoption of the convention) is voluntary,\n125\tout-of-scope per platform policy, and does not gate any epic. The cloudmanager epic may\n126\ttrail the manager epic in release timing (see Health Warning 4) without any DAG change.\n127\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0214ba2b741dc66","agent_id":"a4806c4375d69d57a","session_id":"63a747e2-9d8d-4636-a22b-5d195afcf157","uuid":"4c9ce7e1-1ef8-465c-9e68-be50dc81d9ce","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:42:27.888Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5