vestigation_signals` null everywhere — correct, since\n91\tthere are no Investigation epics. Signal values are consistent with the strategy content\n92\tand the rationales match the frontmatter values. E001's `open_questions: 1` is justified\n93\t(the unverified ~75% figure changes effort, not approach — the audit is in-epic\n94\tfix-and-retry), and E003's `open_questions: -1` is justified (the additional-fields\n95\tquestion directly changes the guideline's content). E003's `human_process_gates: -1` is\n96\tjustified by the docs content process and the mandatory technical review. Calibration\n97\tcheck on `architecture_claims`: all three epics score 1 with rationales citing the actual\n98\tarchitecture-context file (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/\n99\trhods-operator.md`); I verified that file corroborates the controller-runtime/logr\n100\tfoundation, the DSC/DSCI CRDs, and the cloudmanager binary with its three\n101\tcloud-engine CRDs. E001's rationale honestly notes the context file summarizes the\n102\ttiering more coarsely than the SME's 5-tier breakdown without contradicting the surfaces\n103\tin scope. No calibration misses. Computed fields (`ai_implementability`,\n104\t`ai_implementability_score`) were not arithmetic-checked per the review contract.\n105\t\n106\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n107\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria (lint-rule\n108\tmeasurement, unit tests on test logger output, codebase grep, before/after ingestion\n109\tcomparison, reviewer confirmation against shipped code). Rule-mandated ACs: E003\n110\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC (\"a reviewer confirms the before/after\n111\texamples match the converted log calls actually shipped\"); E003's Rule 11 edges carry the\n112\tdocs-last gating. The DSCI key-name migration (a replacement) has its rollback safety\n113\thandled: single-release transition, release-notes documentation with before/after query\n114\texamples, and the both-formats-ingestion AC that makes revertibility verifiable. No\n115\tkonflux-chain epics exist, so the build-pipeline AC is not applicable. No AC states an\n116\texternally-controlled precondition — the dev-cluster log-consumer validation and the COO\n117\ttracking-issue filing are the epics' own deliverables, not availability granted by\n118\tothers. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n119\t\n120\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n121\tCore scope is fully accounted for: all strategy acceptance criteria map to epic ACs\n122\t(conversion, DSCI migration, service-controller resourceKind, 15 action files across 4\n123\ttiers, module controller, cloudmanager, documentation), all four strategy risks are\n124\tcarried forward (coverage checklist + lint for missed call sites, log-consumer regression\n125\tvalidation on both implementation epics, COO publication + tracking issues, DSCI query\n126\tmigration documented in release notes and E003's migration guide), and the open\n127\tquestions are dispositioned in Health Warnings 2-5 rather than silently dropped. The COO\n128\tteam correctly gets no epic (voluntary adoption per overlay 0008, justified in Health\n129\tWarning 6). One minor gap: the strategy's cluster-scoped resource acceptance criterion\n130\t(\"namespace is either omitted or set to empty string ... no spurious namespace value\") is\n131\tcovered explicitly in E001 but not in E002, even though all three cloudmanager kinds\n132\t(AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are\n133\tcluster-scoped CRDs per the architecture context — E002's AC requires `name`, `namespace`,\n134\tand `resourceKind` presence without stating the cluster-scoped namespace handling, so a\n135\ttest written to that AC could pass a spurious namespace. The fix is a one-line AC/scope\n136\taddition to E002 mirroring E001's cluster-scoped bullet; it does not indicate a missing\n137\tepic or a structural defect.\n138\t"}]},