{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8175e25e6debca3c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Manager binary reconciliation log standardization with structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Manager binary reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic converts reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator **manager binary**\n35\tfrom message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs, and adds\n36\t`resourceKind` as a structured field, so operators can query reconciliation errors by\n37\tresource name, namespace, and kind without parsing log message text. It covers all five\n38\tmanager-binary controller tiers: the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, the 8 component\n39\tcontrollers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer,\n40\tTrainingOperator, TrustyAI), the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard,\n41\tAIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator),\n42\tthe DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller, and the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth,\n43\tMonitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup), together with all 15 action files across four\n44\ttiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module). It also migrates the DSCI controller\n45\tfrom non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to the standard\n46\tcamelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), and wires a CI lint /\n47\tstatic-analysis check that keeps the manager binary's log calls conforming after the\n48\tinitial sweep.\n49\t\n50\t## Scope\n51\t\n52\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager\n53\t  binary's controllers and action files (grep/static analysis), building a coverage\n54\t  checklist; confirm the strategy's ~75% embedded-identity estimate against the actual\n55\t  codebase.\n56\t- For every call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract\n57\t  the identity fields and convert to structured KV pairs\n58\t  (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", foo.Name, \"namespace\", foo.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Foo\")`).\n59\t- Migrate the DSCI controller's structured field names from `Request.Name` /\n60\t  `DSCInitialization` to `name` / `namespace`; no references to the old field names may\n61\t  remain.\n62\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all manager-binary reconciliation error\n63\t  logs — including the DSC and the 8 component controllers, where the kind is implied by\n64\t  the controller name but the field enables cross-controller queries (bundled P1\n65\t  requirement; same call sites as the conversion above).\n66\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization)\n67\t  correctly: emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or set to the empty\n68\t  string — never a spurious namespace value.\n69\t- Add unit tests that parse test logger output and assert `name`, `namespace`, and\n70\t  `resourceKind` presence and correctness across controllers, module handlers, and all 15\n71\t  action files.\n72\t- Add a CI lint / static-analysis check that flags any `log.Error` call in the manager\n73\t  binary's controllers and action files that embeds name/namespace in the message text or\n74\t  is missing the required structured fields, wired into CI for the manager binary's\n75\t  surfaces (this epic owns the enforcement for exactly the surfaces it converts).\n76\t- Backwards-compatibility validation per the strategy NFR: before/after log output\n77\t  comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with\n78\t  the production log consumer stack configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify\n79\t  logs are ingested and existing alert queries still match; coordinate with SRE/Support to\n80\t  identify and update any text-based alert rules that rely on message-string patterns.\n81\t- Rollback safety: the DSCI key-name migration lands in a single release (avoiding\n82\t  long-term inconsistency), is documented in release notes with before/after query\n83\t  examples, and the change is revertible with log ingestion verified for both formats.\n84\t\n85\t## Acceptance Criteria\n86\t\n87\t- Given any manager-binary reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource\n88\t  name or namespace in the message string, the updated call uses structured key-value\n89\t  pairs instead — measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis flagging zero remaining\n90\t  `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text across the manager\n91\t  binary's controllers and action files.\n92\t- All DSCI reconciliation error logs use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`,\n93\t  `resourceKind`) — measured by a unit test asserting the standard field names and a\n94\t  codebase grep confirming no references to `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization` remain.\n95\t- Every service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup)\n96\t  reconciliation error log includes `resourceKind` — measured by a unit test parsing test\n97\t  logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value.\n98\t- Every action file error log across all 4 tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module)\n99\t  includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` — measured by a unit test asserting all\n100\t  three fields are present with correct values across all 15 action files.\n101\t- Module controller and module handler error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and\n102\t  `resourceKind` — measured by a unit test on test logger output.\n103\t- DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` —\n104\t  measured by a unit test on test logger output.\n105\t- Cluster-scoped resource reconciliation errors include `name` and `resourceKind` with\n106\t  `namespace` omitted or empty — measured by a unit test confirming no spurious namespace\n107\t  value is emitted.\n108\t- Before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error confirms the\n109\t  structured fields appear and the sample log consumer (e.g., Loki) ingests both formats\n110\t  without configuration changes.\n111\t\n112\t## HLR Traceability\n113\t\n114\t- HLR-1 (P0): all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name or\n115\t  namespace in message strings are converted to structured KV pairs (`name`,\n116\t  `namespace`) — manager-binary portion.\n117\t- HLR-2 (P0): DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names to the camelCase\n118\t  convention.\n119\t- HLR-3 (P0): service controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind`.\n120\t- HLR-4 (P0): all action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`\n121\t  across all 4 tiers (15 action files).\n122\t- HLR-6 (P0): module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`\n123\t  (8 module handlers).\n124\t- HLR-7 (P1, bundled): DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind`\n125\t  for cross-controller queryability — bundled because it is the same edit of the same\n126\t  `log.Error` call sites as HLR-1's conversion; a 